---
import CaseStudy from '../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';

const facts = [
  { k: 'City', v: 'Vienna' },
  { k: 'Year', v: '1996' },
  { k: 'Homes', v: '~112' },
  { k: 'Residents', v: '~200' },
  { k: 'Original', v: 'Coffin factory' },
  { k: 'Tenure', v: 'Self-managed association' },
  { k: 'Public rooms', v: 'Concert hall · restaurant · bathhouse' },
];

const gallery = [
  {
    placeholder: 'Bathhouse — photo needed',
    cap: 'The bathhouse under the courtyard pond',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wien-Penzing%20-%20Wohn-%20und%20Kulturprojekt%20Sargfabrik%20-%2013%20-%20Badehaus.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Haeferl · CC BY-SA 3.0',
    creditHref:
      'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-Penzing_-_Wohn-_und_Kulturprojekt_Sargfabrik_-_13_-_Badehaus.jpg',
  },
  {
    placeholder: 'Chimney — photo needed',
    cap: 'The factory chimney, kept as the landmark',
    src: 'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wien-Penzing%20-%20Wohn-%20und%20Kulturprojekt%20Sargfabrik%20-%2004%20-%20Schornstein.jpg?width=1400',
    credit: 'Haeferl · CC BY-SA 3.0',
    creditHref:
      'https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-Penzing_-_Wohn-_und_Kulturprojekt_Sargfabrik_-_04_-_Schornstein.jpg',
  },
  { placeholder: 'Courtyard — photo needed', cap: 'The courtyard between the rebuilt factory wings' },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="Sargfabrik"
  subtitle="A coffin factory reborn as Vienna's most social address."
  heroSrc="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Wien-Penzing%20-%20Wohn-%20und%20Kulturprojekt%20Sargfabrik%20-%2010%20-%20Dachgarten.jpg?width=1400"
  heroPlaceholder="Sargfabrik hero"
  heroCredit="Haeferl · CC BY-SA 3.0"
  heroCreditHref="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wien-Penzing_-_Wohn-_und_Kulturprojekt_Sargfabrik_-_10_-_Dachgarten.jpg"
  facts={facts}
  gallery={gallery}
  prev={{ href: '/r50/', label: 'R50' }}
  next={{ href: '/brutopia/', label: 'Brutopia' }}
>
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-[760px] px-6 pt-16 sm:px-12">
    <p class="text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      A self-managed association bought Vienna's old coffin factory and rebuilt it as roughly 112
      homes for some 200 people — with a courtyard pond sitting over a bathhouse, a concert hall, a
      restaurant, a children's house, and a roof garden. Crucially, much of that program is open to
      the neighborhood: the Sargfabrik is an address people who don't live there still go to.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-6 text-[21px] leading-[1.62]">
      What to steal: the reuse method. A big roof, bought cheap because nobody else wanted it, can
      carry more social program than any new build pro forma would dare — and the public rooms are
      what turned neighbors into allies.
    </p>
  </section>

  <Fragment slot="lessons">
    <li>
      West Berkeley's warehouses are Sargfabrik sites waiting for a group — reuse carries program
      new builds can't afford.
    </li>
    <li>
      Opening rooms to the neighborhood (hall, restaurant, baths) buys political goodwill no
      rendering can.
    </li>
    <li>
      A self-managed association has run it since 1996 — governance is a solved problem, not an
      experiment.
    </li>
    <li>Keep the chimney: reuse keeps memory, and memory keeps neighbors friendly.</li>
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="sources">
    Sources: <a href="https://www.sargfabrik.at" target="_blank" rel="noopener">sargfabrik.at</a>.
    Figures (~112 homes, ~200 people) are approximate.
  </Fragment>
</CaseStudy>
